# WHAT THE DOCUMENT SAYS ITS REMAINDER IS, BESIDE THE ONE THE ARITHMETIC DERIVES. One row per
# layer that both FILES a remainder and can have one computed from its own `n - d`, carrying
# both figures so a caller can set them against each other.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS THE JOIN THREE CALLERS WROTE OUT SEPARATELY, AND IT IS A RELATION SOMEBODY IN
#    THIS DOMAIN WOULD NAME. `arithmetic/filed_remainder.sqlc`, `matrices.sqlc` and
#    `queries/matrices/4-converted-remainder.sqlc` each spelled `filed_remainders` joined to
#    `remainder` on (filing, layer), in three idioms, for three different questions about the
#    same pair of numbers. The pair is the object; the questions are the callers'.
#
# ⛔ IT IS AN INNER JOIN AND THE ROWS IT DROPS ARE TWO DIFFERENT KINDS OF ABSENCE. A layer that
#   files no remainder at all is not here (layers/filed_remainders.sqlc keeps
#   `remainder_absent IS NULL`), and neither is one whose demand or nameplate is unstated, so
#   nothing could be derived. Both are honest and both are somebody else's question:
#   epistemics/absences.sqlc holds the first, arithmetic/remainder.sqlc the second.
#
# ⭐ THE FILED FIGURE IS NOT THE DERIVED ONE AND MUST NOT BE COALESCED INTO IT. Where a
#   conversion factor multiplies both the nameplate and the demand of a part, the two converted
#   intervals are CORRELATED, and differencing them as if independent counts the factor's spread
#   twice. They agree at the mode and nowhere else. Only the filed figure is the remainder.
-- layers/filed_remainders.sqlc against layers/remainder.sqlc, on the layer they share.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.qty_low, l.qty_mode, l.qty_high, l.qty_unit, l.qty_absent,
       r.r_low, r.r_mode, r.r_high, r.unit,
       r.d_low, r.d_mode, r.d_high,
       r.n_low, r.n_mode, r.n_high, r.amount_unit,
       r.sign, r.derived_fit
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/filed_remainders.sqlc)
) l
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
) r USING (filing, layer)
